    "updated_at": "2025-03-28T23:11:50Z",
    "body": "There are two ways that you can render Declarative Shadow DOM (DSD) on the page:\n\n1. As part of a document HTML\n2. Using JavaScript and `Document.parseHTMLUnsafe()` (which is an unsafe analog of `document.implementation.createHTMLDocument()`) with `document.write` or `setHTMLUnsafe`\n\nThe latter allows you to create client-side DSD, the same way you would do that with document's HTML.\nThe problem is that right now this causes FOUC in Chrome.\n\n| Rendering with document (no FOUC) | Rendering with `document.write` |\n| ------ | ------ |\n| <video src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c17a72af-2f96-4004-a36d-82a6440eb35a\"> | <video src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cde263c7-4a85-453c-baf0-c7dd03d181ad\"> |\n\nThis situation is worsened by the fact that stylesheets are also not cached. Here's a performance trace of a Web Component with DSD rendered 5 times:\n\n<img width=\"535\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/86dc7329-ca5e-4c57-8263-c16b4016c4cd\" />\n\nThis massively impacts the usefulness of DSD and completely negates performance benefits it brings: they get outweighed by the overhead of parsing stylesheets over and over again.\n\nThe FOUC issue is [already reported](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/383593251), caching is also mentioned there.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
